# Elements

## Building elements {#element}

`nml.element(kind, spec)` builds an element. The list part of `spec` holds its children — text, numbers, other elements — and its fields the element's properties.

:example
````
:lua
```
function badge(text)
  return nml.element("bold", {
    classes = { "badge" },
    "[", text, "]",
  })
end
```

Status: :lua`badge("stable")`.
````

Every kind takes `id` and `classes`. The others, by kind:

| Kind | Fields |
|:-----|:-------|
| `paragraph`, `bold`, `italic`, `underline`, `highlight`, `strikethrough` | |
| `heading` | `level`, 1 to 6 |
| `link` | `url` |
| `reference` | `target`: `name`, `page#name`, `page#` or `#name`; children are the caption |
| `code` | `code`, `language` |
| `code_block` | `code`, `language`, `line_offset`, `numbers`, `max_lines`; children are the title |
| `media` | `url`, `type`, `alt`, `width` |
| `figure` | `caption`, `ref`; children are the body |
| `list` | `numbered`, `start`, `bullet`; children are its items |
| `list_item` | `checkbox` (`unchecked`, `partial`, `checked`), `value` |
| `quote` | `author`, `cite`, `url` |
| `callout` | `type`, `title` |
| `layout` | `type`, `title`; each child is a part |
| `raw` | `content`, HTML written as it is; `block` |
| `table` | `caption`, `ref`, `header` (how many header rows), `align`; children are rows, each a list of cells |
| `cell` | `hspan`, `vspan`, `align`: a table cell that needs them |
| `anchor` | `ref`, `title` |
| `math` | `source`, `block` |
| `tex` | `source`, `env` |
| `graph` | `source`, `layout`, `width` |
| `typst` | `source`, `block` |

:example
````
:lua
```
local rows = { { "Lua", "5.4" }, { "NML", "0.1" } }
TABLE = nml.element("table", {
  header = 1,
  { "Name", "Version" },
  table.unpack(rows),
})
```

:lua`TABLE`
````

## Markup {#parse}

A string a script returns is text. `nml.parse(text)` parses it as inline markup instead, with the document's syntax and variables:

:example
```
:lua`nml.parse("**" .. ("ab"):upper() .. "**, [[parse]]")`
```

## Raw HTML {#raw}

Text from scripts is always escaped. HTML that NML cannot write is a `raw` element:

:example
```
:lua`nml.element("raw", { content = "<kbd>Esc</kbd>" })`
```

## Events {#events}

`nml.on(kind, listener)` calls `listener` with every element of that kind that comes after it, before the page is written. The listener reads the element, and can change its `id` and `classes`. To see all of a document's elements, listen from its prologue or from a module, as [[std#toc|std.toc]] does.

:example
````
:lua
```
nml.on("heading", function(h)
  if h.text:find("Draft") then
    table.insert(h.classes, "draft")
  end
end)
```
````

A listener sees the element's `kind`, its `text` without markup, its `id` and `classes`, and what its kind has: a heading's `level`, a list's `numbered` and `start`, an item's `checkbox`, a table's `cells`.
